// TC: O(n)
// we loop through string exactly once it takes O(n) operation.
// Then we loop through fixed-size count array which takes 26 operations(almost constant).
// SC: O(1)
// We are allocating a single integer array of size 26. 

// Follow up: What if the inputs contain Unicode characters? How would you adapt your solution to such a case?

// If the inputs contain Unicode characters, the fixed-size array approach of size 26 will no longer work. Unicode contains over 140,000 characters (including different languages, symbols, and emojis). Allocating a massive array to cover every possible Unicode code point would be highly memory inefficient, as most of the array would remain sparse.

// To adpat the solution we replace the fixed size array with a Hash Table (unordered_map).
// The hash table will dynamically store only the chracters that actually appear in the strings, mapping each character to its frequency count.
// Logic:
// Check if the strings are same length.
// Create an unordered_map to store the char freq
// Iterate through strings, increment the count for char in s and decrement for char in t.
// Iterate through map, if any chracter has a count other than 0, they are not anagrams.

class Solution {
public:
    bool isAnagram(string s, string t) {
        //If the lengths are different, they cannot be anagrams 
        if (s.length() != t.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        //Using hash map to handle any Unicode character 
        unordered_map<char, int> count;

        //Traverse both strings simultaneously
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            count[s[i]]++; //it creates the character if it does not exist and then increments it eg: 't' : 1
            count[t[i]]--; // 'e' : -1
        }

        //Check if any chracter count is non-zero
        for (auto const& [key, value] : count) { //this is the range based for loop chracter is key and integer is value (auto: figures out the data type for us) (const: is we saying to compiler that I am only reading this data I will not change or modify it inside this loop) (&: reference, istead of making a copy it tells the code to look directly at the original data stored in map, saves memory) ([key,value] is the structured binding, Since the map stores a pair, this syntax cleanly unzips the pair into 2 easily readable variables.)
            if (value != 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};

TC: O(n)
Hash map insertion and lookups take O(1) time on average.
We iterate through strings O(n)
We iterate through hash map O(n)
In absolutely worst-case scenario with excessive hash collisions, the time complexity could degrade to O(n^2) but this is extremely rare.

